	abstract = {We develop mean dimension theory for \${\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}\$-flows. We obtain fundamental properties and examples and prove an embedding theorem: Any real flow \$(X,{\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\})\$ of mean dimension strictly less than \$r\$ admits an extension \$(Y,{\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\})\$ whose mean dimension is equal to that of \$(X,{\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\})\$ and such that \$(Y,{\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\})\$ can be embedded in the \${\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\}\$-shift on the compact function space \${\textbackslash}\{f{\textbackslash}in C({\textbackslash}mathbb\{R\},[-1,1]){\textbar}{\textbackslash};{\textbackslash}mathrm\{supp\}({\textbackslash}hat\{f\}){\textbackslash}subset [-r,r]{\textbackslash}\}\$, where \${\textbackslash}hat\{f\}\$ is the Fourier transform of \$f\$ considered as a tempered distribution. These canonical embedding spaces appeared previously as a tool in embedding results for \${\textbackslash}mathbb\{Z\}\$-actions.},
